{
  "response": "# Accessibility Design Review: ProductTabs Component\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:  \nWhile the component demonstrates strong accessibility intent by utilizing semantic HTML buttons, declaring valid ARIA roles (`tablist`, `tab`, `tabpanel`), and setting roving `tabIndex` values, it represents an incomplete (~75%) implementation of the **WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Tabs Pattern**. The roving tabindex interaction model is non-functional because DOM focus is never shifted when arrow keys are pressed, trapping keyboard navigation at the initially selected tab. Furthermore, neither `aria-controls` nor `aria-labelledby` are implemented, severing programmatic relationships between tabs and their corresponding content panels.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nPrior to examining the implementation details, typical failure points for custom tab components include:\n1. **Broken roving focus**: Arrow keys update internal selection state without moving browser DOM focus to the newly active tab button. *(Confirmed)*\n2. **Missing bidirectional bindings**: Tabs lack `aria-controls` targeting panels, and panels lack `aria-labelledby` referencing tabs. *(Confirmed)*\n3. **Unnamed focusable panels**: Tabpanels assigned `tabIndex={0}` lack an accessible name, producing unlabelled focus stops. *(Confirmed)*\n4. **Context-deficient badge announcements**: Numeric counters inside tab buttons announced as naked numbers without semantic explanation. *(Confirmed)*\n5. **Color contrast deficiencies**: Sub-threshold contrast on metadata/status text (e.g., price or inactive tab labels). *(Confirmed)*\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n### 1. Incomplete Roving Tabindex \u2014 Arrow Keys Update State but Never Move DOM Focus\n- **Location**: `ProductTabs.jsx:7-31` (specifically lines 12\u201313, 17\u201318, 21\u201322, 25\u201326)\n- **Element**: `<button role=\"tab\">` and `handleKeyDown`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, switch device users, screen reader users navigating via widget interaction mode.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern (Keyboard Interaction)** and **WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:  \n  `ProductTabs` manages a ref array (`tabRefs.current[index] = el` at line 39), but in `handleKeyDown`, `setActiveTab(newIndex)` is called without invoking `tabRefs.current[newIndex].focus()`.  \n  When a keyboard user presses `ArrowRight`:\n  1. State updates to `newIndex = 1`, re-rendering Tab 0 with `tabIndex=\"-1\"` and Tab 1 with `tabIndex=\"0\"`.\n  2. Because DOM focus is never explicitly moved, the browser retains active focus on Tab 0.\n  3. Subsequent keydown events originate from Tab 0 with `index = 0`. Calculating `(0 + 1) % length` always yields `1`. Keyboard users become permanently unable to navigate past the adjacent tab.\n  4. Visually, this creates a confusing \"split-focus\" state: Tab 1 receives active styling, but Tab 0 retains the `:focus` outline.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tabs-roving-focus-unapplied\nfingerprint: f10a9c8b742e3150\nsource: ProductTabs.jsx:7-31\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern / WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / WCAG 2.0 2.1.1\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM\nevidence: ProductTabs.jsx:12-14: setActiveTab(newIndex) called without tabRefs.current[newIndex]?.focus()\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Focus the first tab using Tab key.\n  2. Press ArrowRight once (active state moves to tab 1, but Tab 0 retains document.activeElement).\n  3. Press ArrowRight a second time. Observe that newIndex recalculates against Tab 0 index, never advancing to Tab 2.\nexpected_behavior: Pressing ArrowRight/Left/Home/End must shift browser DOM focus immediately to the corresponding tab element via tabRefs.current[targetIndex].focus().\nactual_behavior: DOM focus remains on the starting tab button (now tabIndex=\"-1\"), trapping navigation.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n- **Fix**: Update `handleKeyDown` to shift focus immediately after computing the target index:\n```jsx\nconst moveFocus = (newIndex) => {\n  setActiveTab(newIndex);\n  tabRefs.current[newIndex]?.focus();\n};\n\nconst handleKeyDown = (e, index) => {\n  let newIndex;\n  switch (e.key) {\n    case 'ArrowRight':\n      e.preventDefault();\n      newIndex = (index + 1) % products.length;\n      moveFocus(newIndex);\n      break;\n    case 'ArrowLeft':\n      e.preventDefault();\n      newIndex = (index - 1 + products.length) % products.length;\n      moveFocus(newIndex);\n      break;\n    case 'Home':\n      e.preventDefault();\n      moveFocus(0);\n      break;\n    case 'End':\n      e.preventDefault();\n      moveFocus(products.length - 1);\n      break;\n    default:\n      break;\n  }\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n### 1. Missing Programmatic Relationship Between Tabs and Tabpanels (`aria-controls` and `aria-labelledby`)\n- **Location**: `ProductTabs.jsx:40-41` and `ProductTabs.jsx:59-60`\n- **Element**: `<button role=\"tab\">` and `<div role=\"tabpanel\">`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (JAWS, NVDA, VoiceOver)\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)**, **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**, and **WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:  \n  - Each `role=\"tab\"` element lacks `aria-controls={`panel-${product.id}`}`. Screen readers cannot report which region the tab controls, nor can they activate assistive shortcuts (such as VoiceOver `VO + Command + J` or JAWS `Insert + Alt + M`) to jump directly from the tab header into its associated panel content.\n  - Each `role=\"tabpanel\"` element lacks `aria-labelledby={`tab-${product.id}`}`. Because the panel element has `tabIndex={0}` (line 61), it represents a discrete focus stop. When keyboard users tab into it, screen readers announce an unlabelled container: *\"tabpanel\"* rather than *\"[Product Name], tabpanel\"*.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tabs-missing-apg-bindings\nfingerprint: d823e51f479a0b12\nsource: ProductTabs.jsx:37-64\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern / WCAG 1.3.1 / WCAG 4.1.2\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / WCAG 2.0 1.3.1, 4.1.2\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=MEDIUM\nevidence: ProductTabs.jsx:40-41 lacks aria-controls; ProductTabs.jsx:59-60 lacks aria-labelledby\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Inspect the tab and panel elements in the DOM tree.\n  2. Tab into the active panel with a screen reader running.\nexpected_behavior: Tab exposes aria-controls=\"panel-{id}\"; panel exposes aria-labelledby=\"tab-{id}\" so the panel stop announces its context.\nactual_behavior: Neither attribute is rendered; panel is announced without an accessible name.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n- **Fix**: Bind both elements symmetrically using IDs:\n```jsx\n// Line 37: Tab button\n<button\n  key={product.id}\n  ref={el => tabRefs.current[index] = el}\n  role=\"tab\"\n  id={`tab-${product.id}`}\n  aria-controls={`panel-${product.id}`}\n  aria-selected={index === activeTab}\n  tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}\n  onClick={() => setActiveTab(index)}\n  onKeyDown={(e) => handleKeyDown(e, index)}\n  className={`product-tab ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}\n>\n  ...\n</button>\n\n// Line 57: Tabpanel\n<div\n  key={product.id}\n  role=\"tabpanel\"\n  id={`panel-${product.id}`}\n  aria-labelledby={`tab-${product.id}`}\n  tabIndex={0}\n  hidden={index !== activeTab}\n  className=\"product-panel\"\n>\n  ...\n</div>\n```\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (Friction / WCAG Violations with Workaround)\n\n### 1. Insufficient Color Contrast on Product Price\n- **Location**: CSS `.price` line 138 (`color: #059669; font-weight: 600;`)\n- **Element**: `<span className=\"price\">`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Low vision users, users operating under glare or high ambient brightness\n- **WCAG Citation**: **WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) (Level AA)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:  \n  `#059669` rendered against a white (`#ffffff`) background produces a contrast ratio of **3.78:1**.  \n  Because the text inherits the body font size (14px/16px), it does not satisfy the definition of \"large scale text\" (which requires at least 18pt / 24px regular or 14pt / 18.66px bold). Under WCAG 1.4.3, it must achieve at least **4.5:1**.\n- **Fix**: Darken the green value to `#047857` (contrast ratio **4.72:1**) or `#065f46` (contrast ratio **7.01:1**):\n```css\n.price {\n  color: #047857;\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Ambiguous Badge Accessible Name\n- **Location**: `ProductTabs.jsx:49-51`\n- **Element**: `<span className=\"badge\">{product.count}</span>` inside `<button role=\"tab\">`\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users\n- **WCAG Citation**: **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:  \n  Screen readers calculate the accessible name of the button by concatenating all internal text strings. For a product named \"Electronics\" with count `5`, the tab announces: *\"Electronics 5, tab, selected, 1 of 3\"*. Non-sighted users cannot determine whether \"5\" denotes an item inventory count, a category ranking, or a notification identifier.\n- **Fix**: Supplement the badge with visually hidden text or use an `aria-label`:\n```jsx\n{product.count > 0 && (\n  <span className=\"badge\">\n    <span className=\"sr-only\"> (</span>\n    {product.count}\n    <span className=\"sr-only\"> items)</span>\n  </span>\n)}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (Best Practice & Robustness)\n\n1. **Division by Zero Guard in `handleKeyDown` (`ProductTabs.jsx:12`)**:  \n   If `products` is empty or passed as `[]`, `(index + 1) % products.length` evaluates to `NaN`, polluting `activeTab` with invalid state. Add an early return guard: `if (!products || products.length === 0) return;`.\n2. **List Semantics Restoration in Safari/WebKit (`CSS: .product-panel ul`)**:  \n   Applying `list-style: none; padding: 0;` causes VoiceOver on iOS/macOS to strip native list semantics (`role=\"list\"`). To preserve the item count announcement (*\"list, 3 items\"*), add `role=\"list\"` to the `<ul>` element.\n3. **Explicit Tabpanel Focus Ring**:  \n   While `.product-tab:focus` defines an outline, `.product-panel:focus` relies entirely on browser default styling. Adding an explicit `:focus-visible` ring on `.product-panel` reassures keyboard users when focus steps into the panel container.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps & Unstated Assumptions)\n\n1. **Missing `aria-orientation`**: By default, `role=\"tablist\"` is assumed to have horizontal orientation (`aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"`). While this matches the flex row layout here, declaring `aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"` explicitly clarifies assistive technology expectations.\n2. **Missing Focus Restoration / Intent Tracking**: If product items in a panel are deleted or dynamically filtered, focus management does not specify where focus should land.\n3. **No Dynamic Roster Cleanup for `tabRefs`**: Storing element nodes via `ref={el => tabRefs.current[index] = el}` without resetting `tabRefs.current = []` across re-renders can accumulate stale DOM references if the products array shrinks.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|:-----------:|----------------|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Missing `aria-controls`, missing `aria-labelledby`, and focus desynchronization |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | DOM focus not moved by arrow keys; roving tabindex navigation blocked |\n| **Low vision** | **MEDIUM** | Color contrast failure on `.price` (3.78:1); split active vs focused visual indicators |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Trapped tab navigation causes interface freeze perception; unclarified badge numbers |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animations, transitions, or auto-updating elements |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video content present |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **MEDIUM** | Sub-threshold price green fails standard ambient readability requirements |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**:  \n  Without `aria-labelledby` on the panel, entering the panel container announces a bare, disorienting *\"tabpanel\"*. When navigating with arrow keys, virtual cursor and focus remain pinned to the original tab while selection state alters in the background.\n- **Keyboard-only user**:  \n  The interface is fundamentally non-operable past the first tab change. Pressing `ArrowRight` updates selection once, but because physical focus remains on Tab 0, the next `ArrowRight` press continues to evaluate from index 0.\n- **Low vision user**:  \n  The green price text (`#059669`) fails WCAG AA minimum contrast. Furthermore, when arrowing through tabs, the high-contrast focus indicator stays on the previously active tab while the active blue underline shifts to another tab, creating visual dissonance.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**:  \n  The inability to cycle smoothly through categories via predictable arrow key presses violates the standard platform mental model for tabbed widgets.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REVISE**  \n- **Why this verdict**: The component cannot be accepted in its present state because **keyboard accessibility is broken** (WCAG 2.1.1 failure) and **assistive technology associations are missing** (WCAG 1.3.1 / 4.1.2 failure). While the visual design and semantic HTML elements (`<button>`) are solid, the interaction layer is only partially implemented.\n- **Path to ACCEPT**:\n  1. Call `.focus()` on the target tab ref inside `handleKeyDown`.\n  2. Add `aria-controls={`panel-${product.id}`}` to tabs.\n  3. Add `aria-labelledby={`tab-${product.id}`}` to panels.\n  4. Darken `.price` to `#047857` or `#065f46` to satisfy WCAG 1.4.3.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. **Contextual Heading Level**: Inside the panel, items use `<h3>{product.name}</h3>`. Does the surrounding page template have an `<h1>` and `<h2>` preceding this component, or should the heading level be customizable via props (e.g., `headingLevel=\"h2\"`) to avoid skipping levels?\n",
